.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-ENGINE-AUDIT-2026-09-08.md:5:1. **B1 — treatment H/SH is a no-op.** The runner never constructs `InterruptionEvent`s, so `useful_availability ≡ decoding_availability` and 10 of 31 settings are bit-identical duplicates. FIX: interruption events are built from the common event ledger (62 ms same-satellite beam change, 142 ms satellite change; initial entry logged without blackout; overlaps unioned and clipped once, per decision 5 of stage 1) and passed to `score_setting`; KAT: on a world with ≥ 1 handover, `a-γ0 ≠ a-γH` in useful bits and `a-γS ≠ a-γSH`; the setting-duplicate test asserts all 31 receipts are pairwise distinct on a synthetic world with handovers.
.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-ENGINE-AUDIT-2026-09-08.md:6:2. **B2 — factor arms cannot measure component marginals (design CHANGE).** The set decoder's score for FULL/DROP arms is the **sum of the declared targets**, not the exact realised objective: S(config) = C1(config) + C2(config) + C3(config), each the declared target in bits computed by the oracle from the same nominal physics (C1 whole-network difference surplus + Φ; C2 three-offset forecasts with −κ; C3 = interaction share per decision item 5 / Shapley for sets). DROP_Cx removes exactly one term inside the **same selector class and the same catalogue**; `DROP_C3` = no interaction term (joint search kept); an additional `UNI` arm = unilateral-only search with all three terms is reported separately (search value ≠ interaction value); `S_UNI` (stage-4 item 11) is the iterated exact-unilateral comparator. The exact-F oracle is a **ceiling arm** (U1/J1/union certificates), never a factor arm. Marginals are measured on the realised pooled endpoint ΣB/ΣE, never on nominal scores. KATs: on a synthetic world with a constructed non-zero interaction, FULL and DROP_C3 select different configurations and the realised marginal equals the hand-computed value; with zero interaction they coincide; DROP_C1/DROP_C2 differ from FULL on fixtures where the dropped term is decisive.
.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-ENGINE-AUDIT-2026-09-08.md:7:3. **B3 — interference per satellite, colour not bound to beam identity.** Per-chain cross gains (provider decisions 1, 9, 11). Colour is a property of the beam-chain (cell colour in the reuse-3 plan); a user inherits the colour of its serving chain; invariant enforced at tape construction; KAT: the reuse mask is reciprocal (if A interferes with B then B interferes with A) and no chain carries two colours. The bundled `TinySyntheticProvider` is corrected accordingly and the stage-2 dry-run/rehearsal are re-run.
.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-ENGINE-AUDIT-2026-09-08.md:8:4. **B4 — runner cannot execute a real world.** Bounded catalogue (stage-2 decision 4) with an explicit size guard; a user with zero legal candidates receives the null action (unserved) and is excluded from the product — the catalogue never silently collapses to {BASE}; KAT: one user with no legal candidate → 11 non-BASE arms still differ from BASE on a fixture where they should, and a receipt field counts null-action users.
.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-ENGINE-AUDIT-2026-09-08.md:10:6. **Dead producers.** `assert_calibration_world_separation` runs inside `--manifest` and `--calibrate` (KAT: a deliberately colliding domain pair raises); `resolve_configuration` is the single path every arm uses; `project_three_offsets`/`encode_c2_state` either produce rows that are written to receipts or the C2 schema SHA is **not** stamped into matrix receipts (stamp only where rows exist); `assert_reward_core_identity` runs in every unit's `--dry-run`.
